{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Universal Quantifier.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Universal Quantifier.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"universal quantifier universal quantifier","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Universal Quantifier.md","fileName":"Universal Quantifier.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Universal Quantifier","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-16 22:54\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Formal Logic]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Formal Logic","Logically Equivalent","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Universal Quantifier.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Universal Quantifier.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"universal quantifier universal quantifier","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Universal Quantifier.md","fileName":"Universal Quantifier.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Universal Quantifier","heading":"Universal Quantifier","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The universal quantifier ∀ turns an open statement P(x) into the proposition ∀x P(x), which is true exactly when P(a) is true for every entity a in the domain of discourse.\n\nNegating a universal claim produces an existential counterexample: ¬∀x P(x) is [[Logically Equivalent]] to ∃x ¬P(x). The order of mixed quantifiers matters because “one entity works for all” differs from “each entity has some witness.”","tags":["baby"],"links":["Formal Logic","Logically Equivalent","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Universal Quantifier.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Universal Quantifier.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"universal quantifier universal quantifier","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Universal Quantifier.md